 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "BLAENAFON, or Blaenavon. Blaen afon: 'the head of the river'. World Heritage Site since 2000. West: Merthyr Tydfil, over the hill."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "THE MOOR. Heather, sheep and old iron pits. Stay on the path and out of the old workings, bach."
  },
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "blaenavon_name",
   "title": "Blaenavon",
   "text": "Blaenavon means 'the head of the river' (blaen, head; afon, river). The ironworks were sited at the head of the valley because all four things iron needs, ore, coal, limestone and water, lay within a short walk."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "blaenavon_unesco",
   "title": "World Heritage",
   "text": "In 2000 the Blaenavon Industrial Landscape became a UNESCO World Heritage Site, the first in Wales to be listed for its industry. It covers the ironworks, the pits, the quarries, the incline railways and the workers' homes, all in one preserved stretch of hill."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "blaenavon_bigpit",
   "title": "Big Pit",
   "text": "Big Pit was sunk in 1860 and worked as a colliery until 1980. It reopened as the National Coal Museum in 1983, and visitors still go 90 metres down in the cage with a helmet and lamp. The guides are ex-miners. You won't find a better history teacher with a better wheezing cough."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "blaenavon_ironworks",
   "title": "Blaenavon Ironworks",
   "text": "Founded in 1789, Blaenavon Ironworks is one of the best-preserved 18th-century ironworks in Europe. Its furnaces were charged from the top with ore, coal and limestone from the same hillside, and the great water-balance tower lifted trucks from the casting yard without a drop of steam."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "blaenavon_gilchrist",
   "title": "1878",
   "text": "In 1878 a London police-court clerk named Sidney Gilchrist Thomas and his cousin Percy Gilchrist, a chemist at Blaenavon, solved the problem of making good steel from iron full of phosphorus. Their 'basic' process, tried out here, opened the world's phosphoric ores to steelmaking."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "blaenavon_stacksq",
   "title": "Stack Square",
   "text": "Stack Square, built for ironworkers round 1788, still has its rows of cottages looking in on a courtyard. The ironworks owners also ran the company shop and the school. Wages came partly in tokens, which could only be spent in their shop."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "blaenavon_cheddar",
   "title": "Pwll Mawr",
   "text": "Pwll Mawr is Big Pit's Welsh name, 'big pit'. A local cheddar is now matured in the mine's underground tunnels, in the same cool, damp air that preserved miners' sandwiches. It is the only cheese in Britain matured in an old coal mine."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "blaenavon_workmens",
   "title": "The Workmen's Hall",
   "text": "In Blaenavon, as across the valleys, miners paid a few pence from every wage packet to build themselves a hall. Blaenavon's was a library, a reading room, a billiard hall, a stage and a meeting place all at once."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "blaenavon_railway",
   "title": "Pontypool and Blaenavon Railway",
   "text": "A heritage steam railway runs on part of the old mineral line that took coal and iron down to the valley. At nearly 400 metres it is one of the highest standard-gauge railways in Wales, and the weather up there is a character in itself."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 27,
   "plaque": "blaenavon_ponies",
   "title": "Pit Ponies",
   "text": "For about two centuries, ponies hauled the coal trams underground. They lived in underground stables, wore a hard hat of their own, and had names. By the end of the twentieth century machines had replaced them, and the last ponies in Wales retired to fields they had never seen."
  }
 ],
